# Fake agent

<p class="lede">
Hooks, timeline cards and injected MCP tools need agent turns to test, and real providers need credentials, cost
money and never do the same thing twice. The fake agent is a provider plugin that runs one scripted turn per prompt,
with the timeline items real adapters produce.
</p>

```sh
mkdir -p /tmp/ws
npx ppd daemon up --fake-agent
npx ppd paseo -- run --provider fake-agent --cwd /tmp/ws '{"steps":[{"write":{"path":"notes.md","content":"hi"}},{"say":"done"}]}'
```

`--fake-agent` installs the provider ([`assets/fake-agent`](https://github.com/nkwib/paseo-plugin-devkit/tree/main/assets/fake-agent))
into the dev daemon, on start or into a daemon that is already running:

```text
fake agent provider "fake-agent" installed: paseo run --provider fake-agent '<script JSON>'
```

It appears in the app as the provider `fake-agent` with one model, "Scripted". It writes and edits files for real in
the agent's cwd and calls tools on the MCP servers in the agent's session config, which is where plugins inject them
in `before("agent.create")`. Paseo's own mock provider needs a development build and supports no MCP servers.

<Callout kind="tip" title="Workspaces">

`paseo run --cwd DIR` needs DIR to exist and creates a workspace for it, so leave `--workspace` off `daemon up` here.
With two workspaces, `ppd web` needs `--workspace` to know which one to open; pass `--workspace <id>` to
`paseo run` to reuse one.

</Callout>

<h2 id="scripts">What counts as a script</h2>

For every prompt, in order:

1. The prompt contains a fenced block whose language is `fake-agent`. Its body is the script, so the script can sit
   inside a longer prompt.
2. The whole prompt is JSON (it starts with a brace or a bracket): an object with a `steps` array, or a bare array of
   steps.
3. The agent's cwd has a `.fake-agent.json`: turn N runs its Nth script (see [several turns](#turns)).
4. Otherwise the agent answers with one message: `fake-agent received: <prompt>`.

A script from the prompt must parse; an invalid one fails the turn with `fake-agent: invalid script: ...`.

<h2 id="steps">Steps</h2>

Each step has exactly one key. Steps run in order.

| Step | Effect | Timeline item |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `{"say": "text"}` | | `assistant_message` |
| `{"think": "text"}` | | `reasoning` |
| `{"write": {"path", "content"}}` | Writes the file (relative to the cwd), creating directories. | `tool_call` `Write`, `detail: {type: "write", filePath, content}` with an absolute `filePath` |
| `{"edit": {"path", "old", "new"}}` | Replaces the first `old` with `new`. | `tool_call` `Edit`, `detail: {type: "edit", filePath, oldString, newString}`; `failed` when the file or the string is missing |
| `{"bash": {"command", "output"?, "exitCode"?, "run"?}}` | Without `run`, records the command with the output and exit code you give. With `run: true`, runs it (see [shell steps](#bash)). | `tool_call` `Bash`, `detail: {type: "shell", command, cwd, output, exitCode}`; `failed` when the exit code is not 0 |
| `{"mcp": {"server", "tool", "arguments"?}}` | Calls the tool on that MCP server (see [MCP steps](#mcp)). | `tool_call` `mcp__<server>__<tool>`, `detail: {type: "unknown", input, output}`; `failed` on errors |
| `{"sleep": ms}` | Waits. An interrupt cancels the turn. | |
| `{"fail": "message"}` | Ends the turn as failed. | `error` |

`"stepDelayMs": N` next to `steps` pauses N ms before every step, which makes a turn slow enough to watch in the UI.

<h2 id="example">A turn, end to end</h2>

```sh
npx ppd paseo -- run --provider fake-agent --cwd /tmp/ws '{"steps":[
  {"think":"add a constant, then check it"},
  {"write":{"path":"src/a.ts","content":"export const a = 1;\n"}},
  {"edit":{"path":"src/a.ts","old":"1","new":"2"}},
  {"bash":{"command":"cat src/a.ts","run":true}},
  {"bash":{"command":"npm test","output":"1 failed","exitCode":1}},
  {"say":"a is 2 now"}]}'
npx ppd paseo -- logs <agent id>
```

```text
[User] {"steps":[{"think":"add a constant, then check it"},{"write":...},...]}
[Thought] add a constant, then check it
[Write] /tmp/ws/src/a.ts
[Edit] /tmp/ws/src/a.ts
[Shell] cat src/a.ts
[Shell] npm test
a is 2 now
```

`src/a.ts` now holds `export const a = 2;`. The canonical timeline, as a plugin's `agent.turn_ended` hook sees it
(read with `agentTimeline`, below):

```json
{"type":"reasoning","text":"add a constant, then check it"}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"t-1-1","name":"Write","detail":{"type":"write","filePath":"/tmp/ws/src/a.ts","content":"export const a = 1;\n"},"status":"completed","error":null}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"t-1-2","name":"Edit","detail":{"type":"edit","filePath":"/tmp/ws/src/a.ts","oldString":"1","newString":"2"},"status":"completed","error":null}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"t-1-3","name":"Bash","detail":{"type":"shell","command":"cat src/a.ts","cwd":"/tmp/ws","output":"export const a = 2;\n","exitCode":0},"status":"completed","error":null}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"t-1-4","name":"Bash","detail":{"type":"shell","command":"npm test","cwd":"/tmp/ws","output":"1 failed","exitCode":1},"status":"failed","error":"exit code 1"}
{"type":"assistant_message","text":"a is 2 now","messageId":"a-1-5"}
```

A failed tool call does not fail the turn; this one ended `completed`.

<h2 id="outcomes">How a turn ends</h2>

A turn emits `user_message`, then its steps, then ends like a real provider's, so `agent.turn_started` and
`agent.turn_ended` fire as usual:

- `completed`: every step ran.
- `failed`: a `fail` step, an invalid script, or a shell step the daemon refused. `paseo run` shows the agent as
  `error`, and Paseo adds a system error to the timeline:

  ```text
  starting
  [Error] tests failed
  [System Error] tests failed
  ```

- `canceled`: the agent was interrupted, for example with `npx ppd paseo -- stop <agent id>`. A `sleep` ends at once, a running
  command is killed (its tool call fails with `canceled`), an MCP call in flight finishes first, and the remaining
  steps do not run. The agent goes back to `idle`.

<h2 id="turns">Several turns</h2>

A plain prompt in a cwd that has a `.fake-agent.json` runs the next scripted turn. The last one repeats:

```json
{
  "turns": [
    { "steps": [{ "write": { "path": "todo.md", "content": "- one\n" } }, { "say": "wrote todo.md" }] },
    { "steps": [{ "edit": { "path": "todo.md", "old": "one", "new": "one, done" } }, { "say": "updated todo.md" }] }
  ]
}
```

After `paseo run` and two `paseo send`s:

```text
[User] start the todo list
[Write] /tmp/turns/todo.md
wrote todo.md
[User] and again
[Edit] /tmp/turns/todo.md
updated todo.md
[User] once more
[Edit] /tmp/turns/todo.md
updated todo.md
```

This is how you script a conversation from the app's composer, where typing JSON is awkward.

<h2 id="bash">Shell steps</h2>

With `run: true` the command runs with `/bin/sh -c` in the agent's cwd, with a 60 s timeout, and the tool call
reports its real output and exit code. A non-zero exit fails the tool call, not the turn. As with real adapters, a
file the command writes is not attributed to it (no `Write` item).

<Callout kind="warn" title="Only in ppd's daemons">

Real commands run only in a daemon `ppd daemon up` started: the provider checks that the daemon's home carries the
`.ppd-home` marker. Installed into any other daemon, the step is refused without running, the tool call fails with
exit code 126, and the turn fails:

```text
fake-agent refused to run this command: bash steps with run: true work only in a dev daemon started by `ppd daemon up`, and this daemon's home was not created by ppd
```

</Callout>

<h2 id="mcp">MCP steps</h2>

An `mcp` step sends `initialize`, `notifications/initialized` and `tools/call` to the named server from the session
config, over HTTP (JSON or event-stream responses) or stdio, with a 10 s timeout per HTTP request or for the whole stdio
exchange. The tool's `result` is
the tool call's `output`.

- `"server": "*"` calls the tool on every configured server, one tool call each. With none configured it does
  nothing.
- An unknown server is a failed tool call, and the turn goes on:
  `no MCP server "nope" in the session config (have: none)`.

The [server hooks recipe](/docs/recipes/server-hooks) injects an MCP server with `before("agent.create")` and calls
it from a fake turn.

<h2 id="tests">From tests</h2>

```ts
import { agentTimeline, pluginRows, runFakeAgentTurn, sendFakeAgentTurn } from "paseo-plugin-devkit";

const { agentId, status } = runFakeAgentTurn([{ write: { path: "a.ts", content: "x" } }, { say: "done" }], { cwd: workspace });
sendFakeAgentTurn(agentId, [{ mcp: { server: "my-tools", tool: "show_to_human", arguments: { title: "hi" } } }]);
const cards = pluginRows(agentTimeline(agentId), "turn-summary"); // plugin rows, which `paseo logs` omits
```

| Export | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `runFakeAgentTurn(script, { cwd, name, timeoutMs })` | Creates a fake-agent agent and runs one turn to the end (`paseo run --json`). Returns `{ agentId, status }`. |
| `sendFakeAgentTurn(agentId, script, { name, timeoutMs })` | Sends one more turn to an existing agent and waits for it (`paseo send`). |
| `agentTimeline(agentId, { name })` | The canonical timeline, plugin rows included. |
| `pluginRows(timeline, kind)` | The plugin rows of one kind, newest last. |
| `fakeAgentPrompt(script)` | The prompt string for a script, to send from anywhere else. |

`agentTimeline` reads the timeline with the Paseo client inside the installed desktop app (it is not on npm), so it
needs the app or `PASEO_APP`. Plugin rows live in daemon memory, and hooks run asynchronously: poll briefly after a
turn. [`test/create-e2e.test.ts`](https://github.com/nkwib/paseo-plugin-devkit/blob/main/test/create-e2e.test.ts)
is a complete example.
